WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:04.660
Different token types have different purposes and trust boundaries.

2
00:00:04.760 --> 00:00:10.120
A secure .NET application does not treat every token-shaped value the same way.

3
00:00:10.220 --> 00:00:19.020
It asks who issued the token, who is supposed to consume it, what decision it supports, how long it lasts, and which system is allowed to validate it.

4
00:00:19.300 --> 00:00:23.860
Access tokens are intended for resource access decisions by APIs.

5
00:00:23.960 --> 00:00:34.920
An API should validate that the token came from the expected issuer, is intended for the expected audience, is still valid, and carries claims or scopes that the API policy understands.

6
00:00:35.020 --> 00:00:39.020
An access token should not be accepted merely because it is well formed.

7
00:00:39.300 --> 00:00:42.900
ID tokens describe authentication events for clients.

8
00:00:43.000 --> 00:00:48.840
They help a client understand that authentication occurred and who the user is in that client context.

9
00:00:48.940 --> 00:00:52.860
They should not be treated as general API authorization tokens.

10
00:00:52.960 --> 00:00:57.360
Confusing ID tokens and access tokens can blur important trust boundaries.

11
00:00:57.640 --> 00:01:03.240
Refresh tokens support obtaining new access tokens and require stronger lifecycle controls.

12
00:01:03.340 --> 00:01:11.660
They may outlive a short access token and therefore need careful storage, rotation, revocation, expiration, and monitoring.

13
00:01:11.760 --> 00:01:14.720
They should be handled as high-value credentials.

14
00:01:15.000 --> 00:01:23.720
ASP.NET Core Identity may use tokens for password reset, email confirmation, phone change, two-factor flows, and other account workflows.

15
00:01:23.820 --> 00:01:28.540
Antiforgery tokens help validate browser request intent for state-changing actions.

16
00:01:28.640 --> 00:01:34.400
Data Protection-protected payloads represent framework or application-protected data and depend on key management.

17
00:01:34.680 --> 00:01:42.440
Token purpose should be explicit so developers do not validate, store, forward, or log the wrong token in the wrong place.

18
00:01:42.540 --> 00:01:50.220
When purpose is clear, the application can apply the right validation path, lifetime, storage rules, and operational response.
